{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Here is a 300+ word summary of the Wikipedia page \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymond_III,_Count_of_Tripoli\" without commas and with at least 3 highlighted sections in markdown format:\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Saint-Germain and ruled as Count of Tripoli from 1147 to 1177 he was also a claimant to the County of Tripoli\nHe succeeded his father William II Count of Tripoli at a young age and was initially under the regency of his mother Alice of Jerusalem\n*Raymond III played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a strong opponent of King Amalric I*\n\nRaymond III was known for his military campaigns against the Fatimid Caliphate and the Zengid dynasty he launched several attacks on the city of Damascus\nHe also participated in the Siege of Ascalon in 1153 and the Battle of Inab in 1154\nRaymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din Zengi during the Battle of Inab and was held prisoner for nearly a year\n\n*The County of Tripoli under Raymond III experienced a period of relative stability and economic growth despite the constant threats from neighboring Muslim states*\nRaymond III established trade relationships with the Byzantine Empire and the Republic of Pisa\nHe also supported the establishment of the Hospitaller Order in the County of Tripoli\n\nRaymond III never married and died in 1177 without an heir he was succeeded by William of Gaude Mei\n*Raymond III legacy is marked by his military prowess and his ability to maintain the independence of the County of Tripoli during a period of intense conflict* \nHis death marked the end of the Saint-Germain dynasty in the County of Tripoli\nRaymond III is often regarded as one of the most important figures in the history of the County of Tripoli\nHe played a crucial role in the preservation of the Christian states in the Levant during the